\subsection{Preserve the Handler for an Exception after Entry}

An upward transfer can succeed for all ordinary locals while
losing the logical handler stack. Consider a loop inside a
single catch region. At a chosen header the baseline frame
has its caller continuation, one active handler for a
distinguished bounds exception, and an index into a fixed
array. The native variant may throw the same exception on
a load at that header. Use an adapter that associates the
native load's PC with the canonical throwing site and handler
record. Assume the exception does not cross a foreign frame,
there is no cleanup clause, and the runtime already provides an
unwind mechanism for installed code. The adapter does not
reexecute the exception-producing operation.

\begin{sudocode}[Enter with the original exception context]
\State $C\gets$ canonical header state of the current activation
\If{resume identity or handler contract does not match}
  \State \Return continue baseline \Comment{No partial transfer}
\EndIf
\State build native state from $C$ including caller link
\State associate native load site with $C$'s handler scope
\State transfer to native header
\State on native bounds exception at the load:
\State \quad deliver exception at canonical load site
  \Comment{Unwind the transferred activation, not a new one}
\State \quad enter the original catch continuation
\end{sudocode}

Let a loop deliberately read indices 0, 1, and 2 of the
two-element array $(4,5)$, emitting after each successful
read. Its handler emits 99 and returns $-1$. Enter native
code when the index is 1, after emission 4. The native read
at index 1 emits 5; index 2 transfers to the handler, giving
observations $(4,5,99)$ and return $-1$. Test against a
baseline run and check that the caller receives one return.
An adapter for an otherwise identical header outside the catch
must be declined if it cannot implement this handler contract.

The correspondence includes which exception is delivered and
where, not just normal continuation state. Multiple nested
handlers or cleanup routines require additional scope information
and control transfers; one matching local layout cannot justify
skipping them. Check the exception's reported source position as
well as the final handler output.

\section{Choose the points and derive the maps}

D'Elia and Demetrescu's \emph{On-Stack Replacement \`a la Carte} and \emph{On-Stack Replacement, Distilled} formalize correspondences and build compensation code for live state across a class of transformations. A value not live in the old version may still be reconstructible from live inputs and a suitable pure expression; an absent, effectful load is not generally reconstructible that way. Their composition result suggests maintaining per-pass maps rather than reverse-engineering the final machine state, subject to the maps' preconditions. That abstraction does not automatically provide register recovery, unwind or GC support for a concrete runtime.

D'Souza and Duboscq's \emph{Lightweight On-Stack Replacement in Languages with Unstructured Loops} uses backward jumps as an OSR trigger in a Truffle implementation rather than recovering high-level loops first. A backward jump suggests hotness; it does not determine how the frame should be transferred. Essertel, Tahboub and Rompf's \emph{On-Stack Replacement for Program Generators and Source-to-Source Compilers} illustrates another choice: express switches between structured loop variants through continuation functions and carried state. The scan above could use that implementation without a handwritten native stack splice.

Dossier Chapters~02 and~20 and the system blueprint sketch tier transitions and persistent version records. A conservative realization keeps maps to a canonical semantic state instead of maintaining direct maps for every pair of native versions. This only works where both maps describe the same resumable state and the second version's prerequisites can be checked; reject a transition across arbitrary source edits rather than inferring it from matching PCs. Pin source, destination and metadata during transfer and defer reclamation of code until no live stack can refer to it. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-256} covers cases where the canonical continuation has more than one logical frame.
